    {
      "id": "be-s9-trend-c1",
      "heading": "Fitting a linear trend",
      "explanation_md": "Sales (₹ Crore) for 1993 to 1997 are 605, 715, 830, 790 and 835. Number the years t = 1 to 5 and fit **S = a + bt**.\n\n| Year | S | T | T² | ST |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1993 | 605 | 1 | 1 | 605 |\n| 1994 | 715 | 2 | 4 | 1430 |\n| 1995 | 830 | 3 | 9 | 2490 |\n| 1996 | 790 | 4 | 16 | 3160 |\n| 1997 | 835 | 5 | 25 | 4175 |\n| Sum | 3775 | 15 | 55 | 11860 |\n\nThe two **normal equations**: 3775 = 5a + 15b and 11860 = 15a + 55b. Solving gives **b = 53.5** and **a = 594.5**, so **S = 594.5 + 53.5t**.\n\nThe year 2000 is **t = 8**: S = 594.5 + 53.5 x 8 = 594.5 + 428 = **1022.5**. The slide prints 1024.9 on this last line, but the arithmetic gives 1022.5; show your working.",
      "example_ids": [
        "be-ex-linear-trend-1993-1997"
      ],
      "key_terms": [],
      "exam_line": "Build the T, T² and ST columns, solve the two normal equations, then put the forecast year's t into S = a + bt.",
      "clarification": null,
      "check_questions": [
