    {
      "id": "be-s9-trend-c2",
      "heading": "Linear or non-linear? Look at the differences",
      "explanation_md": "Sales for 1990 to 1996 are 10, 15, 22, 31, 43, 57 and 74.\n\n- The **first differences** (5, 7, 9, 12, 14, 17) keep growing, so a straight line will not fit.\n- The **second differences** stay close to **2 or 3**: nearly constant. Nearly constant second differences point to a **quadratic (non-linear) trend**: S = a + bt + ct².\n\nWith seven years the slide codes time as **t = -3 to 3**, with 1993 as 0, so that the sum of t is 0. That makes the equations much easier.",
      "example_ids": [
        "be-ex-quadratic-trend-1990-1996"
      ],
      "key_terms": [],
      "exam_line": "Constant first differences mean a linear trend; constant second differences mean a quadratic trend.",
      "clarification": null,
      "check_questions": [
